<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sechele I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1812–1892</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sechele I was chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bakwena">Bakwena</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tswana-people">Tswana people</a> of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, from the 1830s until his death in 1892. He was baptised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-livingstone">David Livingstone</a> in 1848, the missionary's only recorded convert, and later preached as a Christian in his own right. He survived an attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boers</a> on his town at Dimawe in 1852, made the Kwena a regional power and settled his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molepolole">Molepolole</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sechele-i.jpg" alt="Sechele I" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Theodor Fritsch (1838–1927) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sechele_Gustav_Fritsch_1865.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
